; The target-independent frontend and AST containers (.ai/asm_plan.md §4).
;
; Note what is *not* here: the TARGET signature. This package must not depend on
; target_intf, because §5.1's dependency direction runs the other way - a target
; description depends on the generic machinery and never the reverse. The ASTs
; are parameterized over the target's instruction types rather than functorized
; over TARGET, which is what makes that possible: a container holding an 'insn
; needs no interface to hold it.
;
; Menhir owns the common statement and expression grammar (§4.7) and nothing
; else. Individual instruction forms must never become Menhir productions, so
; operands leave the grammar as token slices.

(library
 (name asm_core)
 (modes byte native)
 (libraries foundation fmt)
 (enabled_if
  (<> %{env:ASM_MELANGE=false} true)))

; Menhir is invoked by an explicit rule rather than by the (menhir ...) stanza,
; because that stanza takes no enabled_if and needs a library to attach to - and
; this library is disabled in the Melange configuration, where the mirror under
; melange/lib/asm_core owns the build instead. Exactly one of the two rules is
; ever enabled, so grammar.ml has exactly one producer and both configurations
; compile a parser generated from this one .mly.

(rule
 (targets grammar.ml grammar.mli)
 (deps grammar.mly)
 (enabled_if
  (<> %{env:ASM_MELANGE=false} true))
 (action
  (run menhir --base grammar %{deps})))
